diff --git a/doc/Manual/ManualAccountsReceivable.html b/doc/Manual/ManualAccountsReceivable.html
index c7bb00d..1a409f2 100644
--- a/doc/Manual/ManualAccountsReceivable.html
+++ b/doc/Manual/ManualAccountsReceivable.html
@@ -201,6 +201,53 @@
 
 <p>As well as unlimited notes on individual customers, webERP has the functionality to store an unlimited number of notes on groups of customers based on the debtor type field. As with the notes on individual customers , these notes are displayed in order of priority, and can contain a URL as well as the free form note.</p>
 <!-- Help End: Customers -->
+<!-- Help Begin: CreditControl -->
+<h2><a id="CreditControl">Credit Control</a></h2>
+
+<p>Credit control applies the same rules to every customer: it reviews credit limits regularly, stops customers who do not pay, chases overdue invoices with escalating reminder letters, charges interest and late fees, and writes off bad debts. Everything is recorded, so for any customer you can see what was decided, by whom, when and why.</p>
+
+<p>Credit control is done by people holding the <b>Credit Control</b> security token (the credit controller, the deputy and the Accountant by default). The Finance Director also holds the <b>Credit Control Authority</b> token, which approves large write-offs and changes the settings. Assign the tokens to roles in Access Permissions. Sales staff can look at credit information for their own customers but cannot change a limit or a credit status.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditStatusWatchStop">Credit status: normal, watch and on stop</a></h3>
+
+<p>A customer's credit status is the Credit Status (hold reason) on the customer record. A status that does not disallow invoices is <b>normal</b>, a status flagged "watch" lets us carry on supplying the customer but warns whoever takes an order, and a status that disallows invoices puts the customer <b>on stop</b>. The statuses available are maintained in Credit Status Maintenance, and the three that credit control uses are chosen in Credit Control Settings.</p>
+
+<p>While a customer is on stop no order is accepted for them by any route - the sales office, the web shop, electronic (EDI or API) orders or standing (recurring) orders - and goods on orders taken before the stop cannot be dispatched or invoiced. Credit control can release an individual order from the Credit Control Worklist. A customer on watch can still order: the person taking the order sees a warning and the order appears on the worklist. The web shop tells a customer on stop to contact us. Customers are never told that they are on watch.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditCustomer">The customer credit page</a></h3>
+
+<p>From Customer Inquiry follow the Credit control link. The page shows the status, limit, balance, overdue amount, last reminder stage, open items with due dates, reminders sent, the review history and the complete credit control history. Credit control can also:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><b>Record a credit review</b>: the outcome (limit confirmed, raised, reduced, put on watch, put on stop, returned to normal), the new limit, notes and the next review date. A new limit takes effect immediately and the review, the reason and the limit change are recorded together. Reviews are never changed or deleted. A new customer gets a next review date automatically, one standard review period after the account is opened.</li>
+<li><b>Change the credit status</b> with a reason. Who and when are recorded.</li>
+<li><b>Mark an invoice as disputed</b>. A disputed invoice is not chased and is not charged interest until the dispute is settled.</li>
+<li><b>Exempt a customer from interest and late fees</b>, and <b>add a note</b> - for example that the debt is with a collection agency or with lawyers.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditWorklist">Credit Control Worklist</a></h3>
+
+<p>Open this every Monday. It lists customers whose credit review is due or overdue, customers over their credit limit and by how much, customers on watch or on stop with the reason, open orders for customers on watch, and orders held for approval. It can be filtered by sales person and by currency, and totals are shown in the home currency. A sales rep sees only their own customers. Orders that would take a customer over their limit are held for credit control approval when Check Credit Limits is set to "Hold for credit control approval" in System Parameters; a held order cannot be dispatched until credit control approves and releases it here.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditReminders">The reminder run</a></h3>
+
+<p>Run Credit Reminder Run (normally weekly). It shows which customers will receive which letter. Untick customers you do not want to chase this time - for example where a payment has been promised - and then send. Letters go by email to the customer's accounts contacts (contacts flagged to receive statements). Customers with no email address are listed with the letter text so you can post it. A customer only moves up a stage while still overdue, never gets the same stage twice, and never gets two letters within the minimum number of days set in the settings. Overdue is calculated after taking off any receipt that has not been allocated yet, and disputed invoices are ignored. Every letter is recorded against the customer, appears on the customer credit page and in the customer portal. The number of stages, the days overdue that trigger each and the wording are set in Credit Control Settings. After the final notice the customer is listed on the worklist for credit control to decide whether to stop them; the system never stops a customer by itself.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditCharges">Month end interest and late fees</a></h3>
+
+<p>At month end run Month End Interest and Fees. Choose the month, check the list of what will be charged to whom and confirm. Interest is the annual rate applied to the balance that is overdue by at least the number of days set in the settings, for the month; the late fee is a flat fee per customer per month (or per overdue invoice). Charges are raised in the customer's own currency, appear on the statement and in the portal as separate items, and post to the income accounts Finance chose. A customer is never charged twice for the same month. To waive a charge, use the waive list on the same screen: a reason is required and the reversal posts to the ledger as well.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditWriteOffs">Bad debt write-offs</a></h3>
+
+<p>Request a write-off in Bad Debt Write-offs, with a reason. Up to the approval limit set by Finance it is posted at once; above it, it waits for the Finance Director, who approves it on the same screen. A posted write-off settles the oldest open invoices, reduces the customer's balance and is posted to the bad debt account. The customer is put on stop unless you untick the box. The same screen reports write-offs over a period with who requested and who approved each. If a written-off debt is later paid, enter the receipt as normal and post a journal to recover the bad debt.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditMonthEnd">Month end: agreeing the ledger to the customer accounts</a></h3>
+
+<p>Interest, fees, waivers and write-offs post to the general ledger and to the customer accounts together. Use Debtors Control Integrity (utilities) to compare the debtors control account with the customer transactions period by period; interest, fees, waivers and write-offs appear in their own column, so any difference can be traced to the period and the kind of transaction.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditSales">A note for sales staff: watch and stop</a></h3>
+
+<p><b>Watch</b> means we still supply the customer but are worried about them: you will see a warning when you take an order, and credit control will see the order. <b>Stop</b> means no new orders can be taken for the customer by anyone, and existing orders will not be dispatched or invoiced until credit control releases them. You can see the status and the reason on the customer credit page, so you can talk to the customer, but you cannot change a status or a limit. Ask the credit controller or the deputy.</p>
+<!-- Help End: CreditControl -->
 <!-- Help Begin: Shippers -->
 <h2>Shippers</h2>
 
